  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1106 PM MST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nCurrent radar shows rain showers ongoing for most of the plains with \nthe heaviest rain over western Morgan County and eastern Adams \nCounty. Some light snow is occurring for the mountains, mainly north \nof I-70. With deep northerly flow still in place, rain/snow showers \nwill remain possible through early Tuesday morning. For the high \ncountry, we expect very little additional accumulation of snow \n(perhaps an inch or two at most), so the Winter Weather Advisory was \ncancelled earlier this evening. Some light rain showers will remain \npossible for the Palmer Divide, eastern plains, and into most of the \nDenver metro. High-resolution guidance (including one of the last \nNAMnest and NAM runs) shows an additional 0.1\"-0.2\" of rain possible \nfor western Washington county and adjacent areas with some isolated \nslightly higher amounts possible through the overnight. The chance \nremains for some snowflakes to mix in for the Palmer Divide to as\nfar north as Akron as well as far northern Weld County given the \ndecreasing snow levels. A dusting is possible for the Palmer\nDivide due to a trend of slightly lower snow levels tonight. The \nchance for precipitation should end between 6am and 9am Tuesday. \n\nGusty winds remain likely on the plains for the next few hours with \nsome gusts of 50-55 mph still possible. Given the gusty winds, there \nis a near zero chance for fog this morning in northern Colorado \nunlike the last several days. Wind gusts of 30-35 mph will remain \npossible on Tuesday afternoon for the eastern plains. Otherwise, \nTuesday will be a temperate day and feature warmer temperatures and \nmore sun than Monday with upper-level ridging in control for our \narea. Highs should reach the mid 50s for the plains.\n\nWednesday brings the next storm system to our area, but it will move \nthrough quickly. A trough is forecast to move across Colorado from \nWednesday afternoon through early Thursday. This system will lack in \nmoisture, so any precipitation looks light in nature. Areas of the \nhigh country along the I-70 corridor and south look to be most \nfavored for any light snow accumulations. Ensemble guidance is in \ngood agreement that these locations should receive 1-3 inches of \nsnow with perhaps some localized higher amounts at the highest \nelevations. DESI probabilities currently show that the chances for \nthe heaviest snow in our area will be in the Mosquito Range. Some \nlight rain showers will be possible for the Palmer Divide and our \nsoutheastern counties with an upslope component as the trough \npasses. The rest of the plains should stay dry. Any precipitation \nlooks to quickly end on Wednesday night. \n\nFrom Thursday onward, ridging builds into our area once again. A \ntrough could move through the upper-level flow to our north on \nFriday. For now, we expect any meaningful precipitation chances to \nremain north of our area as moisture continues to be lacking. \nGuidance is in better agreement that another trough should move in \nfrom Canada down into the Great Plains just to our northeast towards \nthe end of the weekend. This could introduce some gusty winds on \nSunday for the eastern plains if momentum transfer is maximized as \nwinds aloft could be rather strong. Otherwise, Thursday through \nMonday looks dry for our area. Temperatures should remain near \naverage- in the upper 50s for the plains- through at least \nSaturday. The ridge look to strength and build into our area for \nSunday and Monday, so temperatures could even reach the low to mid\n60s for the plains on these days.",
